# Security — Design Authority (Gemeente Den Haag / BIO 2.0)

Sources: [MCSB](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/benchmark/azure/overview) · [BIO 2.0](https://www.bio-overheid.nl/) · [Azure security baseline](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/benchmark/azure/overview)

Defence-in-depth order: **identity → network → compute → data → monitoring**.

## Generated code readability (all languages)

- **No double dashes (`--`)** or em dashes in generated code/comments; use `#`, `//`, or help blocks.
- **Comment each security-relevant block** (identity, network, encryption, logging): what it enforces and which MCSB/BIO theme it supports.
- Flag intentional exceptions with `# ADR-NNNN` or `# CISO exception` in IaC comments.

## BIV / BIO 2.0 classification

- Classify with **BIV**: Vertrouwelijkheid (C), Integriteit (I), Beschikbaarheid (A) = CIA.
- Levels: **Laag / Midden / Hoog** only. Default **Midden** when unsure; confirm Laag/Midden with Data Owner and **CISO delegate**.
- **Hoog BIV → CISO personally** (not delegable).
- Do not under-classify operational security data (MCSB findings, misconfigs, resource IDs) as Laag solely because it is not citizen PII — NIS2/Cbw may require **Midden**.
- LLTD BIV table is a proposal — not a substitute for the formal organisational BIV register.

## Authenticity (A) & non-repudiation (NR)

- Separate from BIV (ISO 27001 / BIO 2 themes). Consider in every design.
- **Include A/NR resource table** when: email to externals, citizen data, financial flows, legal evidence, partner disputes.
- **Omit table** only when Entra/MSI, git/ADO, STRIDE TM-01/TM-03, and §6 CI/CD already suffice — record in Appendix 1.
- Map rows to BIO themes (e.g. 5.17 Authentication, 8.15 Logging, 8.32 Change management).
- NR weak alone: Logic App/Function run history — pair with central immutable logs.

## CISO delegate vs CISO

| Actor | Mandate |
| --- | --- |
| **CISO delegate** | Routine Security Design Review; BIV Laag/Midden; Low/Medium STRIDE residual; scoped platform workloads |
| **CISO (personal)** | Hoog BIV; High §9 risks; mandatory BIO/policy exceptions; public PaaS without PE (ADR); tenant-wide/citizen-facing; NIS2 board-level |

Document delegate name, escalation path, and stage gates in stakeholder tables (§2.1).

## STRIDE threat modeling

- Before architecture finalisation; scopes §4 and pentest.
- Trust boundaries from §3.3a context diagram and §3.4 network/traffic summary.
- Cover: external entry points, layer boundaries, PII locations, admin/pipeline/MSI paths.
- Per threat: scenario, component, mitigation, residual risk, acceptance owner, §9 reference.

## Identity & access

- Staff: Entra ID + MFA; Conditional Access; block legacy auth.
- Citizens: DigiD/eHerkenning via approved broker only — no custom citizen auth.
- Service-to-service: **Managed Identity / workload identity** — no long-lived credentials in code.
- RBAC least-privilege; PIM for elevation with logged activation.
- Key Vault: RBAC; MSI callers; audit secret access.
- Storage: MSI/RBAC or short-lived SAS; no anonymous access on sensitive data.
- APIM: JWT/OAuth; mTLS for partners where required.

## Secrets — never in

Source control, Bicep parameter files, pipeline YAML, standalone Variable Groups, Markdown, logs, or artifacts. Base64 is not encryption.

- Store in **Key Vault**; Variable Groups only when Key Vault-linked.
- Target: **federated OIDC** for ADO service connections (no stored client secrets).
- Rotate immediately on leak. PRs with active secret alerts cannot merge.
- Local dev: SecretManagement module; `.gitignore` local config.

## Secure coding & CI gates (shift-left)

Enable **GitHub Advanced Security** (GHAzDO) on all repos.

**Blocking merge:** no secret violations; no open High/Critical dependency or code-scan alerts; SAST/lint pass; CI green.

Stack: PSScriptAnalyzer + PSRule + Bicep linter on every PR; CodeQL where applicable.

Every alert: fix, documented false positive, or risk accepted (owner + review date).

## Network security

- Prefer hub-spoke, Azure Firewall, NSG default-deny, UDRs, Private Endpoints, TLS 1.2+.
- Ingress: App Gateway + WAF or Front Door. Egress: firewall FQDN allow-lists.
- Admin: Bastion/VPN — no direct RDP/SSH from internet.
- Profile B1 / PaaS-only: traffic summary table when full hub-spoke inventory N/A.
- Prefer identity + Private Endpoint over IP allow-lists.

## Data protection

- TDE/SSE at rest; TLS 1.2+ in transit; no production PII in Dev/Test (mask/s synthesise).
- Log access/mutations; retention ≥180 days operational; immutability where compliance requires.
- Enable diagnostics to central Log Analytics / Sentinel — do not rely on portal-only history.

## Compliance mapping (LLTD §4.4)

Map controls to BIO with verification method (Policy, audit, manual review):

- Access control: RBAC, MFA, PIM
- Logging: central LAW, immutability, retention
- Continuity: RTO/RPO, DR tests
- GDPR: DPIA, DSR workflows (when PII applies)
- Forum Standaardisatie: Digikoppeling/DigiD where citizen/G2G interfaces exist

Policy exceptions and landing-zone deviations → ADR + CISO escalation when mandatory controls are waived.

## IaC security defaults

- System-assigned or user-assigned MSI on Logic Apps, Functions, App Service.
- Disable storage shared keys when MSI path proven (`allowSharedKeyAccess=false`).
- Blob versioning + soft delete where integrity matters.
- ADO: branch policies, PR approval, environment gates on canary/production.
- Grant minimum ARM write scope — constrain blast radius.
- Record security gaps explicitly with backlog items rather than silent omission.

## MCSB & BIO 2.0 (control-plane / IaC)

**Tenant baseline:** GDH MG assigns **Microsoft Cloud Security Benchmark (MCSB)** + **NL BIO Cloud Theme V2**. Effects are the strictest **non-breaking** mix: **Deny** where safe, **Audit** where Deny would break, **DINE/Modify** for auto-remediation. Not all effects in both initiatives are Deny. Legacy MG: same initiatives, **Audit** during migration.

**Policy-as-code:** definitions, initiatives, and assignments in **Bicep**; PR with **≥2 reviewers**; promote via CI/CD; validate all effects (Deny, Audit, DINE, Modify) in **Canary Tenant** before production.

**Before merge/deploy:**
- Pipeline **what-if** + **PSRule for Azure** + **Bicep linter** — do not merge IaC that will hit Deny policies without an approved exception path.
- **GHAzDO merge gate** (all must pass): no secret violations; no open High/Critical dependency or code-scan alerts; SAST/lint green; CI success.

**Code must implement (MCSB-aligned defaults):**
- Credential-free automation: **MSI / federated OIDC** — no long-lived secrets in YAML, jsonc, Bicep params, or PS.
- **TLS 1.2+**, no anonymous public PaaS access, shared keys disabled where MSI is used.
- **Least-privilege RBAC** at narrowest scope (resource → RG → subscription → MG as needed, not tenant-wide write).
- **Logging & change evidence** (BIO 8.15, 8.32): ADO PR history, environment gates, ARM activity logs; diagnostics to central LAW where required.

**BIO themes to reflect in design/code reviews:** 5.15 Access control · 5.17 Authentication · 8.15 Logging · 8.16 Monitoring · 8.24 Cryptography · 8.32 Change management.

**Classification:** security/MCSB operational metadata (findings, resource IDs, scores) = **BIV Midden** typical — not Laag by default (NIS2/Cbw).

**Exceptions:** non-compliant landing-zone posture (public PaaS without PE, policy waiver) → **ADR in §9** + **CISO** sign-off (not delegate alone).

**Segregation of duties:** pipeline author ≠ production release approver; security findings need owner + disposition (fix / false positive / risk accepted with review date).

## MCSB control families (IaC checklist)

When designing or reviewing templates, map resources to [MCSB v2 domains](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/benchmark/azure/overview):

| Domain | IaC must address |
| --- | --- |
| **NS** Network security | PE, disable public access, Azure Firewall/WAF path, TLS 1.2+ |
| **IM** Identity management | MSI, no shared keys, RBAC least privilege, no local admin secrets |
| **PA** Privileged access | PIM for humans; pipeline SPs scoped; no Owner on routine deploy |
| **DP** Data protection | Encryption at rest/transit, soft delete, versioning on sensitive storage |
| **AM** Asset management | Tags, diagnostics, inventory via Azure Policy Audit |
| **LT** Logging & threat detection | Diagnostic settings → central LAW/Sentinel; retention documented |
| **IR** Incident response | Alert rules, actionable logs, runbook reference in LLTD |
| **GS** Governance strategy | Policy assignments, exemptions time-boxed, ADR for waivers |

## Enterprise tenant hardening (beyond single resources)

- **Defender for Cloud** plans enabled per subscription type; regulatory compliance dashboard tracked.
- **Entra ID:** Conditional Access (MFA, compliant device, block legacy auth); protect privileged roles with PIM + approval.
- **Key Vault:** RBAC authorization, soft delete + purge protection in production, audit logs to LAW.
- **Storage:** disable public blob access; `minimumTlsVersion`; immutability policy where audit requires WORM.
- **SQL/PaaS data:** TDE on, `publicNetworkAccess: Disabled` + PE where data class ≥ Midden.
- **Secrets rotation:** documented process; pipeline uses OIDC, not stored credentials.
- **Vulnerability management:** GHAzDO + Defender recommendations triaged; High/Critical blocks merge.
- **Cross-tenant:** canary validates policy assignment mechanics and effects only; prod compliance assessed in prod tenant.

## Sentinel & detection (when applicable)

- Connect data sources to **Microsoft Sentinel** for platform and security ops workloads.
- Use **analytics rules** with entity mapping and clear severity; avoid duplicate detections.
- **Workbooks** for MCSB/BIO compliance posture; no PII in shared queries (see KQL rules in `engineering.mdc`).
- **Automation rules** for ticket routing; document in LLTD §5 observability.

## NL BIO Cloud Theme V2 (Azure Policy)

GDH assigns built-in initiative **NL BIO Cloud Theme V2** (`d8b2ffbe-c6a8-4622-965d-4ade11d1d2ee`, ~278 policies) alongside **MCSB**.

**Important caveats (public audit findings):**

- Initiative maps to **BIO thema-uitwerking Clouddiensten**, not every BIO 2.0 control. Azure Policy covers **technical** configuration only.
- Regulatory Compliance blade shows **partial** compliance; process/organisational BIO controls need separate evidence (procedures, training, DPIA).
- Policy **Audit** reports gaps; **Deny** blocks non-compliant deploys. Do not assume green dashboard = full BIO certification.
- Non-technical BIO themes (supplier management, HR security, physical) remain outside Azure Policy scope.

Reference: [NL BIO Cloud Theme regulatory mapping](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/governance/policy/samples/nl-bio-cloud-theme) · [BIO thema-uitwerking Clouddiensten (CIP)](https://www.bio-overheid.nl/)

## MCSB v2 extended domains (IaC checklist)

MCSB v2 adds domains beyond the core eight. Include in LLTD §4.4 and template review when applicable:

| Domain | Key controls | IaC / ops action |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **PV** Posture & vulnerability | Secure score, Defender recommendations | Enable Defender plans per subscription; triage High in backlog |
| **ES** Endpoint security | EDR, OS hardening | VM extensions, Intune compliance (Profile A with VMs) |
| **BR** Backup & recovery | Backup policies, soft delete | Azure Backup vault + retention; test restore documented in §7 |
| **DS** DevOps security | CI/CD secrets, artifact integrity | WIF/OIDC, GHAzDO gates, signed artefacts where required |
| **AI** Artificial intelligence | AI-1..AI-7 | Approved models, content filters, agent least privilege, HITL, AI red team for Hoog BIV |

Reference: [MCSB v2 overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/benchmark/azure/overview) · [MCSB AI security](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/benchmark/azure/mcsb-v2-artificial-intelligence-security)

## MCSB network & identity controls (high-value)

When reviewing Bicep or LLTD §3.4, explicitly address these MCSB controls:

| Control | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| **NS-1** | Network segmentation (hub-spoke, NSG/ASG boundaries) |
| **NS-2** | Disable public PaaS access; use Private Endpoints |
| **NS-3/NS-6** | Central firewall + WAF on internet ingress |
| **NS-9** | Private connectivity (ExpressRoute/VPN/PE), not public paths for admin |
| **IM-1** | Centralised identity (Entra ID); no local auth for Azure resources |
| **IM-3** | MSI/workload identity for apps and automation |
| **PA-1..PA-8** | PIM, break-glass accounts monitored, no standing Owner for humans |
| **DP-3/DP-4** | Encrypt data in transit (TLS 1.2+) and at rest (CMK or PMK) |
| **LT-3/LT-4** | Diagnostic logs to central store; retention meets BIO 8.15 |

## Encryption & key management

- **Customer-managed keys (CMK):** use when data class ≥ Midden or contractual requirement; Key Vault in dedicated platform RG with PE.
- **Key Vault:** RBAC authorization model; soft delete + purge protection in production; audit logs to LAW.
- **Certificate rotation:** prefer managed certificates (App Gateway, Front Door); document manual rotation runbook if not automated.
- **TLS:** minimum TLS 1.2; disable legacy cipher suites on App Service / Application Gateway.
- **Storage:** infrastructure encryption where supported; immutability (WORM) for audit logs when BIO/NIS2 requires tamper evidence.

## Zero Trust alignment (Azure)

Apply in design reviews alongside MCSB:

1. **Verify explicitly** — Conditional Access, MFA, continuous access evaluation where licensed.
2. **Least privilege** — RBAC at narrow scope; JIT via PIM; pipeline identities per stage.
3. **Assume breach** — Segment networks; central egress inspection; Sentinel detections on identity and data plane.
4. **Validate explicitly** — Device compliance for admin paths; signed commits / branch policies for IaC repos.

Reference: [Zero Trust on Azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/security/zero-trust/azure-infrastructure)

## Policy exemptions & exceptions

- **Exemption** in Azure Policy: time-boxed, named owner, business justification, linked ADR for Deny waivers.
- **CISO path:** public PaaS without PE, disabled diagnostics on production tier, shared key access on sensitive storage.
- **Never** permanent exemption without review date; include `expiresOn` in configuration parameters.
- Document exemption in Git (configuration governance folder) when policy-as-code is source of truth.

## NIS2 / Cbw considerations (platform metadata)

- Operational security data (Defender findings, misconfigurations, subscription inventory) often **BIV Midden**, not Laag.
- Incident reporting obligations may apply to essential/important entity workloads; document IR contacts in LLTD §5.
- Supply chain: third-party SaaS/PaaS in scope needs vendor assessment (BIO supplier theme); Azure shared responsibility model in §4.4.

## Security review checklist (PR / LLTD)

Before merge or Security Design Review sign-off:

- [ ] BIV classification confirmed (Hoog → CISO personal)
- [ ] STRIDE completed for new trust boundaries
- [ ] No secrets in code, params, YAML, or logs
- [ ] MSI/OIDC for all automation identities
- [ ] Public network access disabled or ADR exception
- [ ] Diagnostics → central LAW configured in IaC
- [ ] PSRule + Bicep linter + GHAzDO green
- [ ] MCSB/BIO policy effects tested in canary where Deny is new
- [ ] Authenticity/NR table present when external/citizen/financial evidence flows exist
